    "files": [
      {
        "description": "This is the OpenSesame/OSWeb script used to run the color-shape switching task.\n\nIn the color-shape switching task, participants were presented with red and blue squares and circles, which appeared one-by-one in the middle of the screen on a gray background (RGB: 155, 155, and 155). Depending on the criterion, the participant had to either decide on the color or the shape of the stimulus by pressing a key. The cues, which only appeared in mixed blocks, were the Dutch words “VORM” (English: ‘shape’) or “KLEUR” (English: ‘color’) and appeared in white in Arial (font size: 72) in the middle of the screen. Four different versions of the color-shape switching task were used in a counterbalanced manner, which differ in the order of task blocks and the hand participants use to respond to the different blocks. Trials were presented as follows. First, the participants looked at a fixation cross at the center of the screen for 350 ms. In the single-task blocks, the stimulus appeared after a lag of 150 ms. Alternatively, in mixed blocks, a cue (“KLEUR” or “VORM”; 250 ms) appeared for 250 ms preceding the stimulus and remained on screen during the response window (max. 4000 ms). In the mixed blocks, trials of the same type (switch or non-switch) did not appear more than four times in a row. In total, participants completed 144 single-task block trials (72 color and 72 shape task trials) and 144 mixed trials (72 switch and 72 non-switch trials).",
